.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt GETSOCKNAME 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm getsockname ,
.Nm getpeername
.Nd get a socket's local or peer address
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.Ft int
.Fn getsockname "int fd" "struct sockaddr *restrict addr" "socklen_t *restrict addrlen"
.Ft int
.Fn getpeername "int fd" "struct sockaddr *restrict addr" "socklen_t *restrict addrlen"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn getsockname
returns the local address of socket
.Fa fd
in
.Fa addr .
For an IPv4 stream or datagram socket, the result is a 16-byte
.Vt sockaddr_in
containing the TCP control block's local IPv4 address when one exists,
and otherwise the address the socket bound, 0.0.0.0 for any; a connected
datagram socket bound to any address reports the address its datagrams
leave from, as Linux does.
The port is the socket's bound or ephemeral port.
An accepted connection reports the address the peer connected to, and a
connection to 127.0.0.1 reports 127.0.0.1.
.Pp
For an
.Dv AF_PACKET
socket,
.Fn getsockname
returns a 20-byte
.Vt sockaddr_ll
with the socket's protocol, bound interface index, Ethernet hardware
type, and that interface's six-byte MAC address.
An unbound socket or one bound to every interface reports index zero
with hardware type and address length zero.
.Pp
.Fn getpeername
returns a connected peer's IPv4 address and port in a
.Vt sockaddr_in .
A stream socket has a peer after the TCP handshake from
.Xr connect 2
or
.Xr accept 2
has completed, including the subsequent closing states while its
connection record remains attached.
A listener, a socket still completing its handshake, or a closed
connection has no peer to report.
A datagram socket has a peer after
.Xr connect 2
has set one.
.Pp
Before either call,
.Fa addrlen
must point to the capacity of
.Fa addr
in bytes.
The kernel copies at most that capacity and writes the full address
length back through
.Fa addrlen ,
even when the address is truncated.
Both pointers must be nonnull, including when the capacity is zero.
In an IPv4 result, the family is in host byte order and the port and
address are in network byte order.
.Pp
These operations are subcalls 6 and 7, respectively, of i386
.Xr socketcall 2 ,
system call 102.
.Pp
On x86-64
.Nm getsockname
is call 51 and
.Nm getpeername
52.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return 0 on success.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/loopprobe.c
uses
.Fn getsockname
to obtain the local IPv4 address.
After connecting to a local TCP listener, it uses
.Fn getpeername
to check that the peer has that address and the listener's port.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EOPNOTSUPP]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not an open descriptor.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
Either pointer is null, the address length cannot be read or written,
the address cannot be copied to user memory, or the
.Fn socketcall
argument array cannot be read.
.It Bq Er ENOTCONN
.Fn getpeername
was called on a socket with no connected peer.
.It Bq Er ENOTSOCK
.Fa fd
does not refer to a socket.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
The socket is a raw socket.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr accept 2 ,
.Xr bind 2 ,
.Xr connect 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr socketcall 2
